          <groundspeak:finder id="548391">G &amp; E</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP268 William A. Switzer Provincial Park&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Over two years since this cap had a visitor and I can't see any reason for it.  It's easy to find and we drove right up to it!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="431317957">
          <groundspeak:date>2014-07-30T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="2319799">CanadianRockies</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP268 - William A. Switzer Provincial Park

Posted: N53 25.163 W117 44.627

MyGPSr: N53 25.162 W117 44.630&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Found this one about 3 feet in front of its blue metal guard post and about 1 inch above ground level. It was clean. Thanks for the cap.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="319229682">
          <groundspeak:date>2013-06-01T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="836983">Coolcat21</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP268 William A Switzer Provincial Park&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;MyGPSr:  N53 25.164 W117 44.629

Elevation: 1333 m&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With a 4x4 I would have been able to make this is drive up, but I didn't trust my car on the wet ground so I parked on the “main” road and walked in the 100 and some meters to this easy find. This cap is in very good shape.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the cap!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="301048652">
          <groundspeak:date>2013-03-27T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1298988">dronnord</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP268 &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;William A Switzer Prov. Park  &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;N 53 25.164 W 117 44.627 Elev = 1340&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The snow was hip deep with a crust and sugary snow underneath. The snowshoes were required but the TWO nose dives were unexpected,not required and laughed about.....later...&lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile_big.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;TFTBCP and the exercise&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="214645106">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-02-22T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="664594">Sears Tower</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP268 - William A. Switzer Provincial Park&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After finding Octopus Lake cap, I had planned on going to dinner, but my co-worker/newbie cacher wanted to do some more caps!  The closest ones were in Hinton, so off we went.  By the time we got to this cap location, it was dark.  We walked through the deep and crusty snow with our shovel and metal detector.  You just have to love caps that stick above the ground!  TFTH&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
